ng x1 is 0. But the point B is on the smaller circle, x1^2 + y1^2 = 1, so x1=0 implies y1=±1. Similarly, for the larger circle, if C is on it, then x2 would be 4 (since line O2C would be along x-axis, but O2 is at (4,0)). Wait, this could be a possible approach. Let\'s suppose BC is vertical. Then, point B has x-coordinate 0, which is the center of the smaller circle. Wait, but the smaller circle is at (0,0), so a vertical line through O1 would be x=0. Then, the points of tangency would be (0,1) and (0, -1). But then, the external common tangent would be those points. However, the problem states that the circles touch each other externally at A. If the vertical line x=0 is the tangent, then the points of tangency would be (0,1) and (0,-1). But then, the points of contact would be at (0,1) and (0,-1), but the problem states that they touch externally at point A. So this suggests that point A is (0,1) or (0,-1). However, the problem mentions that the two circles touch each other externally at point A. If the tangent line is x=0, then the points of tangency are (0,1) and (0,-1), which would imply that the circles touch at (0,1) and also at (0,-1). But that would mean two points of contact, contradicting the problem statement which says they touch externally at one point A. Therefore, the tangent line cannot be vertical. Hence, BC is not vertical.\n\nAlternatively, since BC is a common external tangent, and the only other possibility is a tangent that is oblique. So let\'s get back to the equations. Let me consider coordinate geometry.\n\nGiven two circles:\n\n- Circle 1: center at (0,0), radius 1.\n\n- Circle 2: center at (4,0), radius 3.\n\nWe need to find points B(x1, y1) on Circle 1 and C(x2, y2) on Circle 2 such that the line BC is tangent to both circles.\n\nThe line BC is tangent to Circle 1 at B, so the radius O1B is perpendicular to BC. Therefore, the slope of O1B is y1/x1 (since O1 is at (0,0)), so the slope of BC is -x1/y1 (negative reciprocal).\n\nSimilarly, the radius O2C is perpendicular to BC, so the slope of O2C is y2/(x2 - 4), and hence the slope of BC is - (x2 - 4)/y2.\n\nThus, both slopes must be equal:\n\n- x1/y1 = - (x2 - 4)/y2\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nx1/y1 = (x2 - 4)/y2\n\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\nx1 y2 = (x2 - 4) y1\n\nAdditionally, since B is on Circle 1:\n\nx12 + y12 = 1\n\nAnd since C is on Circle 2:\n\n(x2 - 4)^2 + y22 = 9\n\nSo we have the system of equations:\n\n1. x1 y2 = (x2 - 4) y1\n\n2. x12 + y12 = 1\n\n3. (x2 - 4)^2 + y22 = 9\n\nWe need to solve for x1, y1, x2, y2.\n\nLet me try to express y1 and y2 in terms of x1 and x2 using equations.\n\nFrom equation 1:\n\nx1 y2 = (x2 - 4) y1 => y2 = y1 (x2 - 4)/x1\n\nFrom equation 2:\n\nx12 + y12 = 1 => y1 = sqrt(1 - x12) or y1 = -sqrt(1 - x12). Similarly for y2.\n\nHowever, since points B and C are on the upper halves of their circles (as the circles are touching externally at A and the tangent is in the upper half), y1 >=0 and y2 >=0. So we can take y1 = sqrt(1 - x12) and y2 = sqrt((x2 - 4)^2 + y12 - 9). Wait, equation 3 is (x2 - 4)^2 + y22 =9, so y2 = sqrt(9 - (x2 -4)^2). Thus, both y1 and y2 are positive due to the circles\' upper halves.\n\nSo, substituting y2 from equation 1 into equation 3:\n\n(x2 - 4)^2 + [ sqrt(9 - (x2 - 4)^2) ]^2 = 9\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n(x2 - 4)^2 + 9 - (x2 - 4)^2 = 9\n\nThis holds true for all x2, so it doesn\'t give us any new information. Therefore, our equations are not independent. Hence, we need another approach.\n\nAlternatively, using parametric coordinates. Let me parametrize points B and C.\n\nLet’s parametrize point B on the smaller circle (radius 1) as (cosθ, sinθ), since it\'s centered at (0,0). Similarly, parametrize point C on the larger circle (radius 3) as (4 + 3cosφ, 3sinφ), since the center is at (4,0) with radius 3.\n\nThe line BC is tangent to both circles. The tangent line at B to the smaller circle is perpendicular to O1B, which has a direction of (cosθ, sinθ). Therefore, the tangent line at B has a direction perpendicular to that, i.e., (-sinθ, cosθ). Similarly, the tangent line at C has a direction (-3sinφ, 3cosφ) since O2C direction is (cosφ, sinφ), so tangent direction is (-sinφ, cosφ), scaled by 3.\n\nBut since BC is the common tangent, its direction is the same as the direction of the tangent at B and tangent at C. Therefore, the direction vector of BC is (-sinθ, cosθ) and also (-3sinφ, 3cosφ). Therefore, these two direction vectors must be scalar multiples of each other.\n\nHence:\n\n- sinθ / (-3 sinφ) = cosθ / (3 cosφ)\n\nWait, direction vectors are proportional, so:\n\n(-sinθ, cosθ) = k (-3 sinφ, 3 cosφ)\n\nfor some scalar k.\n\nThus:\n\n- sinθ = -3k sinφ => sinθ = 3k sinφ\n\ncosθ = 3k cosφ\n\nDivide the two equations:\n\ntanθ / 3 = (3k cosφ) / (3k sinφ) => tanθ / 3 = cotφ => tanθ / 3 = 1 / tanφ => tanθ * tanφ = 3.\n\nSo, tanθ * tanφ = 3.\n\nAdditionally, points B and C are:\n\nB = (cosθ, sinθ)\n\nC = (4 + 3 cosφ, 3 sinφ)\n\nSo the vector from B to C is (3 cosφ - cosθ, 3 sinφ - sinθ). The direction of this vector is the same as the direction of the tangent line BC, which is (-sinθ, cosθ). Therefore, the vector BC is a scalar multiple of (-sinθ, cosθ). Therefore:\n\n(3 cosφ - cosθ, 3 sinφ - sinθ) = m (-sinθ, cosθ)\n\nfor some scalar m.\n\nWhich gives us two equations:\n\n3 cosφ - cosθ = -m sinθ\n\n3 sinφ - sinθ = m cosθ\n\nWe can solve these equations for cosφ and sinφ.\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\n3 cosφ = -m sinθ + cosθ => cosφ = (cosθ - m sinθ)/3\n\nFrom the second equation:\n\n3 sinφ = m cosθ + sinθ => sinφ = (m cosθ + sinθ)/3\n\nRecall that for any angle, cos^2φ + sin^2φ = 1. Therefore, substitute cosφ and sinφ:\n\n[ (cosθ - m sinθ)/3 ]^2 + [ (m cosθ + sinθ)/3 ]^2 = 1\n\nExpand:\n\n( cos^2θ - 2m cosθ sinθ + m^2 sin^2θ + m^2 cos^2θ + 2m cosθ sinθ + sin^2θ ) / 9 = 1\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n[ cos^2θ + sin^2θ + m^2 (cos^2θ + sin^2θ ) ] / 9 = [1 + m^2] / 9 = 1\n\nTherefore:\n\n(1 + m^2)/9 = 1 => 1 + m^2 = 9 => m^2 = 8 => m = ±2√2. \n\nSo m is ±2√2.\n\nAdditionally, from earlier, tanθ * tanφ = 3.\n\nRecall that m comes from the relation between tanθ and tanφ. Let me recall that:\n\nFrom the direction vectors, we had tanθ * tanφ = 3.\n\nAlso, m was derived as ±2√2.\n\nAlternatively, let me express m in terms of tanθ and tanφ.\n\nWe have:\n\ncosφ = (cosθ - m sinθ)/3\n\nsinφ = (m cosθ + sinθ)/3\n\nThen, tanφ = sinφ / cosφ = [m cosθ + sinθ]/[cosθ - m sinθ]\n\nSo tanφ = [m cosθ + sinθ]/[cosθ - m sinθ]\n\nBut tanθ = sinθ / cosθ\n\nAnd from tanθ * tanφ = 3:\n\ntanφ = 3 / tanθ\n\nTherefore, setting equal:\n\n3 / tanθ = [m cosθ + sinθ]/[cosθ - m sinθ]\n\nLet me substitute m = 2√2 (taking positive first, the negative case may lead to same relations due to symmetry). Let\'s pick m = 2√2.\n\nSo:\n\n3 / tanθ = [2√2 cosθ + sinθ]/[cosθ - 2√2 sinθ]\n\nLet’s set tanθ = t. Then sinθ = t cosθ. So:\n\n3 / t = [2√2 cosθ + t cosθ]/[cosθ - 2√2 t cosθ]\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by cosθ:\n\n3 / t = (2√2 + t)/(1 - 2√2 t)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n3 (1 - 2√2 t) = t (2√2 + t)\n\n3 - 6√2 t = 2√2 t + t2\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\nt2 + (6√2 + 2√2 ) t - 3 = 0\n\nt2 + 8√2 t - 3 = 0\n\nSo quadratic equation in t: t = [ -8√2 ± sqrt( (8√2)^2 + 4*3 ) ] / 2\n\nCalculate discriminant:\n\n(8√2)^2 = 64 * 2 = 128\n\n+ 4 * 3 = 12\n\nTotal discriminant = 128 + 12 = 140\n\nsqrt(140) = 2 sqrt(35)\n\nHence, t = [ -8√2 ± 2√35 ] / 2 = [ -8√2 + 2√35 ] / 2 = -4√2 + √35 or t = [ -8√2 - 2√35 ] / 2 = -4√2 - √35\n\nSince t = tanθ must be real number. So both solutions are valid but we need to check which one is applicable.\n\nGiven that points B and C are on the upper halves of their circles, so sinθ and sinφ are positive. Also, cosθ and cosφ are also positive since they are in the upper halves. Alternatively, not necessarily. If the circles are in the upper half-plane, points B and C have positive y-coordinates. However, in the case of parametrization, angles θ and φ could be in the upper half or not. The important thing is that points B and C 